# Target C-II — higher-degree free reflection apexes force a third lower gate

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only strengthening of the FREE-MODULE gate branch  
**Scope:** FREE reflection modules whose unloaded shallow apex has full contact degree at least four  
**Dependencies:** free-module two-gate amplification; C26 unloaded-degree bound `3 <= deg_C <= 5`; C65 full diameter visibility; connected positive contact support  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_free_module_degree_gate_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The two-gate theorem used one inactive-guard absorption. The same argument can be iterated one more time whenever the reflected shallow apex has contact degree at least four. Only a bounded number of guards is moved, so every absorbed guard remains in the same `O(sqrt(D))` canonical support cap and the upper-distance proof is unchanged up to constants.

Let `d=deg_C(x)`. Canonical C26 gives `3 <= d <= 5`. For one FREE reflection module define

\[
U=\{z\in A:|z-q|>D\},\qquad
L=D+\delta,\qquad
t_D=\delta+D^{-1/3},
\]

with

\[
0<\delta<h_*={\sqrt{303}\over52}.
\]

If `d >= 4`, then before the reflected apex becomes diameter-feasible one of the following must occur:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{CORE-GATE:}&\text{one moving blocker/absorbed guard reaches an original contact-core vertex;}\\[1mm]
\text{TRIPLE-GATE:}&\text{at least three distinct lower gate edges are created, all between}\\
&\text{vertices that were fully inactive in the original source.}
\end{array}}
\tag{DG.1}
\]

Every underlying original source pair in those gate edges satisfies

\[
\boxed{1<|uv|\le1+t_D<1.3347479844+o(1).}
\tag{DG.2}
\]

Thus degree-three is the only FREE-apex class for which two inactive lower gates can be the first surviving finite obstruction.

---

## 1. Event-and-absorption process

Start with the blocker set `U` translating by `-N`. At a lower event, collect every moving/fixed pair that has just reached unit distance.

If any fixed endpoint has positive old contact degree, CORE-GATE holds and the process stops.

Otherwise every fixed endpoint participating in the event was contact-isolated in the canonical original source. C65 therefore makes it actual-diameter-isolated as well. Absorb all such fixed endpoints into the moving cluster and continue them with velocity `-N`, preserving every newly formed gate contact exactly.

Stop as soon as three distinct gate edges have been created. This is TRIPLE-GATE. Consequently the only case needing a terminal contradiction is one in which at most two gate edges have appeared.

Because each event that does not stop contributes at least one new gate edge, at most two inactive-guard absorption stages are needed before the theorem has already concluded.

---

## 2. Two absorptions remain in the canonical support cap

The original blocker support depths obey

\[
a_z:=H-N\cdot z\le B_D=O(\sqrt D).
\]

Suppose an inactive guard `v` is absorbed when it first contacts a currently moving point `p`. If `p` joined the moving cluster at time `sigma_p`, then at event time `s`

\[
|p-(s-\sigma_p)N-v|=1.
\]

Therefore

\[
a_v\le a_p+(s-\sigma_p)+1\le a_p+t_D+1.
\tag{DG.3}
\]

After at most two absorption stages,

\[
\boxed{a_v\le B_D+2(t_D+1)=O(\sqrt D)}
\tag{DG.4}
\]

for every absorbed guard.

The one-guard upper-safety argument depends only on this `O(sqrt(D))` support-depth bound and on total inward displacement below `t_D<1/2`. Hence it applies verbatim to each of these finitely many absorbed guards. No absorbed guard can create a new distance above `D`; pairs internal to the moving cluster are constant after the later endpoint joins.

Every absorbed guard also stays strictly inside diameter `D` from `q` at terminal time, because

\[
N\cdot(v-q)=L-a_v=D-O(\sqrt D)>0
\]

and its subsequent displacement is inward along `-N`.

Thus, if the process reaches `t_D` with at most two gate edges and without CORE-GATE, the exact tooth reflection `x -> q` is feasible.

---

## 3. Terminal contact count rules out zero, one, or two gates when `d >= 4`

Let `k` be the number of gate edges present at terminal time. Under the case being considered,

\[
0\le k\le2.
\]

Every endpoint of every gate edge was fully inactive in the original source. No old contact incident to those vertices existed, and no unrecorded new contact can appear: such a contact would itself have been a lower event.

The reflection preserves exactly the two base contacts at `x` and deletes the other `d-2` old contacts incident to `x`. Hence

\[
|C'|=|C|-(d-2)+k.
\tag{DG.5}
\]

If `d >= 5`, then `d-2 >= 3`, so `k <= 2` gives

\[
|C'|<|C|.
\]

The terminal source is triangle-free because at most two new gate edges were created and `q` has only its two nonadjacent base contacts. This contradicts primary contact minimality among triangle-free global minimizers.

If `d=4`, then `d-2=2`.

- For `k<=1`, (DG.5) again gives fewer contacts.
- For `k=2`, the contact count is unchanged. A graph with only two new gate edges cannot contain a triangle. At least one of those edges forms a nontrivial contact component disjoint from the component containing `q,a,b`, because all gate vertices were old contact isolates and no CORE-GATE occurred.

So the terminal source is another contact-minimal triangle-free global minimizer whose full contact graph has at least two nontrivial components. C-I makes all of its contacts strong; a relative-interior normalized stress then has positive support on the whole contact graph, contradicting canonical connected positive support.

Therefore terminal feasibility with at most two gate edges is impossible whenever `d>=4`. This proves (DG.1).

---

## 4. Source-wide degree split

Let the FREE side of the module-anchor dichotomy contain

\[
F_D>\left({c_*\over2}-o(1)\right){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D},
\qquad c_*=0.129220199818177\ldots .
\]

Split these modules by shallow-apex degree.

Either at least `F_D/2` have degree three, giving a degree-three FREE population of size

\[
\boxed{\left({c_*\over4}-o(1)\right){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}}
\tag{DG.6}
\]

or at least `F_D/2` have degree at least four. In the latter case, applying (DG.1) and one further two-way split gives either CORE-GATE or TRIPLE-GATE on at least

\[
\boxed{\left({c_*\over8}-o(1)\right){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}}
\tag{DG.7}
\]

modules, where

\[
{c_*\over8}=0.016152524977272125\ldots .
\]

The module separation still makes these bounded gate gadgets vertex-disjoint across retained modules.

**Disposition:** proved that every degree-four/five FREE reflection apex needs a core contact or at least three inactive lower gates. The only two-gate endpoint left is degree three, where the tooth has exactly one non-base old contact. C-II remains open.
